# Line endings.
#
# Without this file, Git relies on each machine's core.autocrlf. On Windows
# that is typically `true`, which checks files out as CRLF while storing LF —
# so every commit prints "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches
# it", and the working tree ends up a mix of both.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the working tree to LF on every platform, matching what is
# already stored. The site is built by Node tooling and deployed to a Linux
# runner, so LF everywhere is what the toolchain expects anyway.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files must never be normalised — Git would corrupt them looking for
# line endings to convert.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.zip   binary
